WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.090
Lesson 2.1: AI-Assisted Dramatic Storytelling.

2
00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.409
GENAI 101 - Module 2: Theater.

3
00:00:08.409 --> 00:00:15.047
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.647 --> 00:00:21.820
In this lesson you'll write a two-character scene with a
text model as your writing partner.

5
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:32.556
You'll write a brief, get a first draft, direct two rounds
of revision with specific notes, and then rewrite at least a
third of the dialogue in your own voice.

6
00:00:32.556 --> 00:00:38.863
The finished scene, marked to show your changes, goes into
the gallery ready for a table read.

7
00:00:39.463 --> 00:00:44.250
A text model is a fast, tireless, slightly bland writing
partner.

8
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.932
Its first draft will be competent and forgettable.

9
00:00:47.932 --> 00:00:52.866
What makes it useful is a brief that carries your artistic
choices.

10
00:00:52.866 --> 00:00:56.843
Who are the characters and what is their relationship?

11
00:00:56.843 --> 00:00:59.641
What does each one want in this scene?

12
00:00:59.641 --> 00:01:01.335
What stands in the way?

13
00:01:01.335 --> 00:01:06.196
Where are we, what should it feel like, and how long should
it be?

14
00:01:06.196 --> 00:01:15.917
Objective meeting obstacle is the core of dramatic writing,
and the model doesn't know what your characters want unless
you tell it.

15
00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:21.010
After the first draft, your prompts become director's notes.

16
00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.931
Good notes are specific and actionable.

17
00:01:23.931 --> 00:01:43.626
'Cut the exposition in the first six lines and start with
her already holding the trumpet case.' 'Give him one line
where he almost agrees, then retreats.' 'Replace the final
speech with a single gesture.' Vague notes like 'make it
better' produce vague rewrites.

18
00:01:43.626 --> 00:01:47.071
You'll give three notes per round, two rounds.

19
00:01:47.071 --> 00:01:50.666
There's a note bank on the lesson page to adapt.

20
00:01:51.266 --> 00:01:55.464
A scene is finished when it sounds like people you know.

21
00:01:55.464 --> 00:02:01.986
Models default to characters who say exactly what they mean
and explain their feelings.

22
00:02:01.986 --> 00:02:08.133
Real people deflect, interrupt, joke at the wrong moment,
and leave things unsaid.

23
00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:16.978
Your rewrite is where the scene becomes yours: cut
explanation, add subtext, and give each character one verbal
habit.

24
00:02:16.978 --> 00:02:20.876
Track your changes so a reader can see what you did.

25
00:02:20.876 --> 00:02:30.697
Then add a title, a character list, and a one-line note to
actors, and post it with your brief, your six notes, and
your rationale.

26
00:02:30.697 --> 00:02:35.494
Read it aloud before you post; you'll hear things you can't
see.
